\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n+1}}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{5}^{n}{n}^{n}\\cdot n}{(9+5n)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{9+5n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\sqrt[n]{n}\\cdot \\frac{5n}{n})}{(\\frac{9}{n}+\\frac{5n}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(\\frac{9}{n}+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1}{0+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=1,$ the root test gives no conclusion." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30cdb164-81fa-5575-aede-39cb02771ca3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534994 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{11}^{n}}{{7}^{n}\\cdot n!})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{11}{7\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{n!}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{7}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{n!}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a25fcb4-6a85-5416-8ab8-d6107a3d8b17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534998 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{n}^{n/2}}{3{n}^{{n}^{2}}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[n]{3}\\cdot {n}^{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{n-1/2}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{\\frac{1}{3}}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=0<1,$ the series converges by the root test." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af92a5d6-20ee-53fa-be9c-28e2a23392fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535055 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(10n)^{n}n}{(8n+1)^{n}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{8n+1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10n\\sqrt[n]{n}}{n(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{10\\sqrt[n]{n}}{(8+\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{8+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{5}{4}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $L={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ for the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.$ According to the root test, does ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ converge or diverge?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d497f3f8-01e4-568d-82c0-af0842ace04d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535069 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}\\ge 0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and that $L$ is defined as  \n\n$$\nL={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, the root test states the following:\n\n- If $L<1$, the series converges.\n\n- If $L>1$ or $L=\\infty $, the series diverges.\n\n- If $L=1$, the root test is inconclusive.\n\nWe have the sequence ${a}_{n},$ defined as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nTo apply the root test, we find the limit of $\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}}$ as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{({a}_{n})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{{6}^{n}}{{5}^{n}{n}^{6}})}^{1/n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6}{5\\cdot \\sqrt[n]{{n}^{6}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[n]{n}})}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\cdot {1}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[n]{n}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $L=\\frac{6}{5}>1,$ the series diverges by the root test." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535095 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "114b601e-a2a6-516f-83df-76e39dceef0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535095 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535105 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535105 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39e29671-6707-57da-9854-b346b8c2d709" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535105 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186535069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535113 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8979fcef-655b-5dad-8545-0b736d277a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535124 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535124 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9351eac-8615-5d5f-97f7-1246fb7f128b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535124 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535128 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186535113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535128 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c7f6c44c-a20c-524b-85ce-89426189d976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535128 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186534994 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
